    "Now a letter comes from her lender, an offer to refinance, a new loan at a slightly lower rate that pays off the old one for $5 ,124 in fees. How big a rate cut makes that worth it for Nora? And where would your own loan fall? And why would a smaller mortgage need a bigger cut?",
    "By month 24, she owes $1 ,133 more than if she had kept the old loan. If she sold the house then, that $1 ,133 would come out of her sale money, so it cancels part of what she has saved. Her savings need a few more months to cover it, and the gap keeps growing a little while they do. Count it and break even moves from month 24 to month 30."
